WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.160
A sodium atom has 11
electrons around its nucleus.

2
00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:05.600
You can't watch them.

3
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:08.200
You can't take a picture
of where they sit.

4
00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:11.880
Yet chemists confidently
write them as two, then two,

5
00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:15.580
then six, then one,
in shells and subshells.

6
00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:20.280
So how can shining light on atoms reveal
how their electrons are arranged?

7
00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:27.300
In this mission, you'll use Coulomb's law: how charge
and distance set the pull between nucleus and electron,

8
00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:30.660
and how inner electrons shield outer ones.

9
00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:33.540
Then you'll fire high-energy
photons at atoms,

10
00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:37.780
knock electrons out, and learn to
read the spectrum they produce.

11
00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:43.470
Your mission: explain how a beam of light can
reveal the arrangement of an atom's electrons.